1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:05,950
This is a timeline we built from the
sources of the American Civil War:

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
not only the four years of fighting, but
the forty years of crisis before them

3
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:15,770
and the twelve of Reconstruction after.
A hundred and twenty-one moments,

4
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:20,214
forty durations, forty links, and
each names where it comes from.

5
00:00:21,894 --> 00:00:27,334
Separate by tags, choose slavery, and
the cause runs through all of it.

6
00:00:27,384 --> 00:00:33,244
The Missouri Compromise in
1820. Nat Turner's rebellion.

7
00:00:33,294 --> 00:00:37,384
Frederick Douglass's Narrative.
The Fugitive Slave Act,

8
00:00:37,434 --> 00:00:40,984
Kansas–Nebraska, Dred
Scott, and on the far side,

9
00:00:41,034 --> 00:00:42,526
emancipation.

10
00:00:44,266 --> 00:00:48,856
Zoom into the 1850s and
the crises come faster.

11
00:00:48,906 --> 00:00:54,306
Uncle Tom's Cabin. Kansas
opened to slavery in 1854,

12
00:00:54,356 --> 00:01:00,666
and seven years of fighting there. In
1857 the Supreme Court rules that Black

13
00:01:00,716 --> 00:01:07,051
Americans can't be citizens.
In 1859, John Brown's raid.

14
00:01:08,601 --> 00:01:15,111
Lincoln is elected in November 1860
without a single Southern electoral vote.

15
00:01:15,161 --> 00:01:20,791
Six weeks later South Carolina secedes,
and by February seven states have left

16
00:01:20,841 --> 00:01:26,031
and six have founded the Confederacy,
all before he takes office.

17
00:01:26,081 --> 00:01:28,889
After Fort Sumter, four more follow.

18
00:01:30,599 --> 00:01:36,059
The seceding states said why. South
Carolina cites the North's hostility to

19
00:01:36,109 --> 00:01:42,119
slavery. Mississippi says its position
is thoroughly identified with it.

20
00:01:42,169 --> 00:01:48,009
And that March the Confederacy's vice
president calls slavery its cornerstone.

21
00:01:48,059 --> 00:01:51,219
Open any moment to read
it, with its sources.

22
00:01:52,889 --> 00:01:56,739
The war itself is a band, from
Fort Sumter to Appomattox:

23
00:01:56,789 --> 00:02:01,819
four years less three days.
The commands are bands too.

24
00:02:01,869 --> 00:02:05,339
McClellan leads in the
East for fifteen months.

25
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:12,526
Lee commands for almost three years. Grant
takes every Union army in March 1864.

26
00:02:14,216 --> 00:02:18,486
Separate the West, and you can see
where the Union was gaining ground:

27
00:02:18,536 --> 00:02:23,516
Fort Donelson and Shiloh in
1862, Vicksburg in 1863,

28
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:28,116
Chattanooga, Atlanta, the
march to the sea. In the East,

29
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:31,867
the armies fought back and
forth across Virginia.

30
00:02:33,417 --> 00:02:39,307
Zoom into 1862 and follow the
links. Antietam, in September:

31
00:02:39,357 --> 00:02:43,087
about twenty-three thousand men
killed, wounded or missing,

32
00:02:43,137 --> 00:02:47,927
the bloodiest single day in
American history. Five days later,

33
00:02:47,977 --> 00:02:52,197
the preliminary Emancipation
Proclamation, and on New Year's Day,

34
00:02:52,247 --> 00:02:54,399
the proclamation itself.

35
00:02:56,129 --> 00:03:02,499
July 1863 holds the turning
point. Three days at Gettysburg.

36
00:03:02,549 --> 00:03:07,009
Vicksburg surrenders on the Fourth,
Port Hudson five days later,

37
00:03:07,059 --> 00:03:11,769
and the Mississippi is the Union's.
Then New York's draft riots,

38
00:03:11,819 --> 00:03:15,269
where mobs hunt Black New
Yorkers, and two days after,

39
00:03:15,319 --> 00:03:18,537
the 54th Massachusetts at Fort Wagner.

40
00:03:20,237 --> 00:03:26,857
Pull out another thread: Black soldiers.
Frederick Douglass calls men to arms.

41
00:03:26,907 --> 00:03:31,997
Harriet Tubman guides a Black regiment up
the Combahee River and frees more than

42
00:03:32,047 --> 00:03:36,037
seven hundred and fifty people.
Fort Pillow, the Crater,

43
00:03:36,087 --> 00:03:41,497
New Market Heights. About a hundred and
seventy-nine thousand Black men serve in

44
00:03:41,547 --> 00:03:43,117
the Union army.

45
00:03:44,847 --> 00:03:52,687
April 1865. Richmond falls on the third.
Lee surrenders at Appomattox on the

46
00:03:52,737 --> 00:03:56,408
ninth. Five days later, Lincoln is shot.

47
00:03:58,118 --> 00:04:02,638
But Appomattox wasn't the end.
Johnston surrenders in April,

48
00:04:02,688 --> 00:04:07,028
Kirby Smith in June, Stand
Watie, a Cherokee general,

49
00:04:07,078 --> 00:04:10,878
on the twenty-third. Four days
before him, at Galveston,

50
00:04:10,928 --> 00:04:13,998
an order tells Texas that
all slaves are free:

51
00:04:14,048 --> 00:04:18,648
Juneteenth. The last surrender
is a ship, the Shenandoah,

52
00:04:18,698 --> 00:04:20,200
in November.

53
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:26,550
Open the war's own band for its cost:
about six hundred and twenty thousand

54
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:31,570
soldiers dead by the traditional count,
about seven hundred and fifty thousand

55
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:37,624
by a 2011 census-based
estimate. Both are estimates.

56
00:04:39,344 --> 00:04:42,494
Then three amendments: the
Thirteenth, ending slavery,

57
00:04:42,544 --> 00:04:48,154
the Fourteenth and the Fifteenth.
Reconstruction is a band of twelve years,

58
00:04:48,204 --> 00:04:54,303
ending in 1877, when the last federal
troops leave the Southern statehouses.

59
00:04:56,033 --> 00:05:00,100
Open it, follow the links, and
see where every date comes from.
